<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Colac sits far enough west of Geelong that asbestos gets handled differently here, and the awkward part starts with the tip. Plenty of the town's houses, and most of the older farm buildings around Colac Otway, went up while asbestos cement was the cheapest sheet on the market. It was standard in Australian residential building from the mid-1940s until the late 1980s, and homes built up until 1990 routinely used it. Fibro walls and eaves, corrugated sheeting on a dairy or a machinery shed, a grey fence line along the boundary.</p><p>We are not a removalist and we hold no licence. What we do is get the material tested and put you in touch with licensed asbestos removalists working in this area, and their WorkSafe Victoria licence number appears on every quote. You contract with them directly, so the price and the timing sit between you and them. If the sample comes back negative, and that does happen, you have spent the cost of a lab test instead of a removal.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Colac</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Colac Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 8px;">Testing first, before anyone touches it</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The sample is analysed by a NATA-accredited laboratory, so being in Colac rather than Geelong barely matters for this step. Most houses need three to eight samples, because a fence, an eaves lining and a bathroom sheet count as three materials, not one.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 8px;">Fibro fences and farm outbuildings</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Old boundary fences, dairies, hay sheds and workshops around Colac were commonly clad in asbestos cement. A full fence line or a whole shed is well past both of the limits the law sets for unlicensed removal work, which is the honest reason those jobs go to a licensed removalist.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 8px;">Asbestos cement roofing</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Corrugated asbestos cement roofing is still on plenty of houses, garages and sheds out this way, and decades of weather leave it brittle. Storm damage is the usual reason a roof suddenly becomes urgent, and nobody should be walking on it to take a look.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 8px;">Where the waste can legally go</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Asbestos cannot go in a kerbside bin, a hard waste collection or the local transfer station, and Colac Otway Shire has no site licensed to receive it. When a licensed removalist does the work, the waste leaves with them, which is the simplest answer for most people.</p>
      </div>
</div>
